Q: Effective hiking - dynamic programming A: <p>Let <code>dp[i][j]</code> represent the minimum sum of longest trails using <code>i</code> days to cover the first <code>j</code> trails. A naïve dynamic programming transition would try all possible split points on each day, which would indeed be <code>O(D * N^2)</code> (where <code>D</code> is the number of days and <code>N</code> is the number of trails).</p>
<p>However, when calculating <code>dp[i][j]</code> we do not need to consider all indexes <code>k &lt; j</code>, but only indexes that are the maximum value in some segment that extends to <code>j - 1</code>. At the current index <code>j</code>, we either create a segment with the value at <code>j</code> being the largest, or <code>j</code> is covered by a previous segment with a larger value.</p>
<p>The value at index <code>j</code> can cover all elements not greater than it, so it subsumes all previous segments until the segment with the nearest larger value. For all segments it subsumes, we can calculate the new result by subtracting the previous segment maximum from the previous segment result and adding the value at index <code>j</code>. Then, we also need to consider the minimum result from all segments with a greater maximum.</p>
<p>The useful segments may be maintained with a monotonic stack for an overall time complexity of <code>O(ND)</code>. It is only necessary to maintain the dynamic programming values for the previous day and the current day for <code>O(N)</code> space.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public static int minTrails(List&lt;Integer&gt; trails, int days) {
    var dp = new int[trails.size()];
    dp[0] = trails.get(0);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; dp.length; i++) dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i - 1], trails.get(i));
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; days; i++) {
        var nextDp = new int[dp.length];
        var stk = new ArrayDeque&lt;Integer&gt;();
        for (int j = i; j &lt; dp.length; j++) {
            nextDp[j] = trails.get(j) + dp[j - 1];
            for (; !stk.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; trails.get(stk.peek()) &lt;= trails.get(j); stk.pop())
                nextDp[j] = Math.min(nextDp[j], nextDp[stk.peek()] - trails.get(stk.peek()) + trails.get(j));
            if (!stk.isEmpty()) nextDp[j] = Math.min(nextDp[j], nextDp[stk.peek()]);
            stk.push(j);
        }
        dp = nextDp;
    }
    return dp[dp.length - 1];
}
</code></pre>
